# =============================================================================
# .trivyignore — Temporary Trivy suppressions
# =============================================================================
#
# Rules:
#   1. Every entry MUST have an expiration date (exp:YYYY-MM-DD).
#   2. Every entry MUST link to an upstream tracking issue/advisory.
#   3. When an entry expires, re-evaluate: remove it if the CVE is fixed,
#      or extend the date with a new justification if the fix still isn't
#      available. Never extend without re-checking upstream.
#   4. Keep the file short. A growing ignorefile is a smell.
#   5. Verify against the pinned CI Trivy (TRIVY_VERSION in security.yml /
#      cve-scan.yml) and its DB source — local `brew install trivy` uses
#      the ghcr.io DB which can classify severities differently from the
#      CI's mirror.gcr.io/aquasec/trivy-db:2.
#
# Format:
#   CVE-XXXX-XXXXX exp:YYYY-MM-DD
# =============================================================================

# CVE-2026-25680, CVE-2026-25681, CVE-2026-27136, CVE-2026-33814,
# CVE-2026-39821, CVE-2026-42502, CVE-2026-42506 — golang.org/x/net v0.49.0,
# statically linked into the pinned kubectl binary (ARG KUBECTL_VERSION in
# Dockerfile.dev; the identical v1.36.3 is fetched by
# lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile). Surfaced by security:trivy:container-scan
# on the GCO dev image and the helm-installer Lambda image — the only two
# scanned images that carry kubectl. Fixed in x/net 0.53.0 (CVE-2026-33814)
# and 0.55.0 (the other six).
#
# Not yet clearable by a pin bump: v1.36.3 is the latest stable kubectl
# (https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable-1.36.txt) and the whole release-1.36
# branch still pins x/net v0.49.0. The bump to x/net >= 0.55.0 currently lives
# only on kubernetes master, so it lands in a future kubectl patch, not a
# release we can pull today.
#
# Low runtime risk: the flagged surfaces are not reached by kubectl acting as
# an HTTP/2 client against a trusted EKS API server — x/net/html.Render
# (CVE-2026-25680 / -25681 / -27136 / -42502 / -42506) renders HTML the client
# never produces, x/net/idna punycode (CVE-2026-39821) is off the API path,
# and the HTTP/2 SETTINGS-frame DoS (CVE-2026-33814) is a server-side parse
# while kubectl is the client. The dev image is a contributor/CI tool that is
# never deployed.
#
# Clears when a kubectl release ships with x/net >= 0.55.0: bump KUBECTL_VERSION
# in Dockerfile.dev and the pin in lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile, then drop
# these entries. Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-25680 (and the
# matching nvd/cve-2026-XXXXX page for each ID above).
CVE-2026-25680 exp:2026-09-22
CVE-2026-25681 exp:2026-09-22
CVE-2026-27136 exp:2026-09-22
CVE-2026-33814 exp:2026-09-22
CVE-2026-39821 exp:2026-09-22
CVE-2026-42502 exp:2026-09-22
CVE-2026-42506 exp:2026-09-22

# CVE-2026-34040 — github.com/docker/docker v28.5.2 (Moby authorization
# bypass), statically linked into the docker-buildx CLI plugin that
# Dockerfile.dev installs (ARG BUILDX_VERSION). Surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on the GCO dev image — the only scanned image
# that carries the Buildx plugin. Fixed in docker/docker 29.3.1.
#
# Not yet clearable by a pin bump: re-checked 2026-07-30 against buildx
# v0.36.0 (now the pinned release) — its go.mod still requires docker/docker
# v28.5.2. The same v0.36.0 bump did clear the four suppressions that used to
# sit beside this one: the containerd trio CVE-2026-53488/-53489/-53492
# (v0.36.0 pins containerd v2.3.3, past the 2.3.2 fix), the Go-toolchain
# CVE-2026-39822 (the v0.36.0 release binary reports go1.26.5), and the
# gRPC-Go GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf (v0.36.0 pins grpc v1.82.1, the fixed version).
#
# Low runtime risk: buildx is a build-time CLI plugin, not a running moby
# daemon, so the daemon authorization-bypass CVE is off the path buildx
# exercises (registry-to-registry image copy and BuildKit builds). The dev
# image is a contributor/CI tool that is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when a buildx release ships rebuilt against docker/docker >= 29.3.1:
# bump BUILDX_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (the monthly deps-scan tracks it) and
# drop this entry. Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-34040
CVE-2026-34040 exp:2026-09-22

# CVE-2026-14257 (GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg) — brace-expansion denial of service via
# unbounded expansion length, causing an out-of-memory process crash. Present in
# the copy npm vendors inside its own node_modules
# (usr/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion) in the dev image, so
# it is surfaced only by security:trivy:container-scan on Dockerfile.dev. Fixed
# upstream in brace-expansion 5.0.8.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the advisory was published 2026-07-24
# and reached the Trivy DB after the last green main run, so it appeared on an
# unchanged dev image.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: re-checked 2026-07-30 against npm 12.0.2 (the
# release this ARG now pins) — it still bundles brace-expansion 5.0.7,
# verified in the installed tree
# (node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion/package.json). The bundled
# copy lives inside npm's published tarball, where neither `npm audit fix`
# nor an `overrides` entry can reach it.
#
# Low runtime risk: the expansion path is reached through brace globs in npm's
# own CLI argument handling, driven by the contributor or CI invocation rather
# than untrusted remote input. The dev image is a contributor/CI tool and is
# never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors brace-expansion >= 5.0.8:
# bump ARG NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (plus the packageManager pins the
# consistency check holds in lockstep) and drop this entry.
# Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-14257
CVE-2026-14257 exp:2026-09-22

# CVE-2026-69152 (GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895) — brace-expansion denial of service via
# unbounded intermediate arrays, bypassing the CVE-2026-14257 mitigation
# suppressed above. Published 2026-08-01, fixed upstream in brace-expansion
# 5.0.9. Same vendored copy as CVE-2026-14257: npm ships brace-expansion 5.0.7
# inside its own node_modules in the dev image, surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on Dockerfile.dev. The aws-cdk-lib bundled
# copy in the root package-lock.json (5.0.8 after the 2.263.0 bump) is also
# in the affected range; npm-audit tracks that one via the matching
# GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895 entry in .npm-audit-ignore.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: checked 2026-08-04 — npm 12.0.2 (ARG
# NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev) is the latest npm release on any dist-tag
# and still bundles brace-expansion 5.0.7; no aws-cdk-lib release bundles
# 5.0.9 yet. Both copies live inside published tarballs where neither
# `npm audit fix` nor an `overrides` entry can reach them.
#
# Low runtime risk: same exposure profile as CVE-2026-14257 above — the
# expansion path is driven by contributor/CI-authored brace globs, not
# untrusted remote input, and the dev image is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors brace-expansion >= 5.0.9:
# bump ARG NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (plus the packageManager pins the
# consistency check holds in lockstep) and drop this entry, along with the
# matching .npm-audit-ignore entry once aws-cdk-lib re-bundles too.
# Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-69152
CVE-2026-69152 exp:2026-09-22

# CVE-2026-69192 (GHSA-mwp4-54f8-5fhr) — ip-address parses leading-zero IPv4
# octets as decimal while OS resolvers treat them as octal, enabling
# SSRF/trust-boundary bypass in code that validates an address string and
# then hands it to a resolver. Fixed upstream in ip-address 10.3.1. Present
# only as ip-address 10.2.0 vendored inside npm's own node_modules
# (socks-proxy-agent -> socks -> ip-address) in the dev image, surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on Dockerfile.dev.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: checked 2026-08-04 — npm 12.0.2 (ARG
# NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev) is the latest npm release on any dist-tag
# and still bundles ip-address 10.2.0 inside its published tarball, out of
# reach of `npm audit fix` and `overrides`.
#
# Low runtime risk: the flagged path only runs when npm connects through a
# SOCKS proxy (npm's `proxy` config), and the address it parses is the
# operator-configured proxy host — not untrusted remote input. The dev image
# is a contributor/CI tool that is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors ip-address >= 10.3.1: bump
# ARG NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (plus the packageManager pins the
# consistency check holds in lockstep) and drop this entry.
# Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-69192
CVE-2026-69192 exp:2026-09-22

# CVE-2026-56852 — golang.org/x/text at v0.33.0, statically linked into the
# pinned kubectl binary (ARG KUBECTL_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev; the identical
# v1.36.3 is fetched by lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile). Surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on the GCO dev image and the helm-installer
# Lambda image. Fixed in x/text 0.39.0.
#
# Not yet clearable by a pin bump: re-checked 2026-07-30 — v1.36.3 is still
# the latest stable kubectl on any release line
# (https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt). The docker-buildx half of this
# entry cleared with the bump to buildx v0.36.0, which pins x/text v0.40.0;
# kubectl is now the only binary carrying the flagged module.
#
# Low runtime risk: the flaw is a denial-of-service only — norm.Iter enters
# an infinite loop on invalid UTF-8 input (golang/go#80142). kubectl here
# processes operator-authored manifests, not untrusted remote input, and both
# images are contributor/CI/deploy tooling rather than long-lived network
# services.
#
# Clears when a kubectl release ships with x/text >= 0.39.0: bump
# KUBECTL_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev and the kubectl pin in
# lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile, then drop this entry.
# Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-56852
CVE-2026-56852 exp:2026-09-22

# CVE-2026-50163 (GHSA-77gc-fj98-665h) — oras.land/oras-go/v2 v2.6.1
# information disclosure / arbitrary file access via crafted tarball
# hardlinks, statically linked into the pinned Helm binary
# (helm-v4.2.3-linux-amd64, lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile). Surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on the helm-installer Lambda image — the
# only scanned image that carries helm. Fixed in oras-go v2.6.2.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the advisory reached the Trivy DB
# after the last green main run and appeared on an unchanged helm binary.
#
# Not yet clearable by a pin bump: checked 2026-08-05 — v4.2.3 is the
# latest helm release on both active lines (v3.21.3 likewise) and both
# still pin oras-go v2.6.1; the bump to v2.6.2 is merged on helm master
# only, so it lands in a future helm patch release.
#
# Low runtime risk: the flagged path is OCI-registry chart *pull*
# extraction. The helm-installer Lambda pulls exactly the four
# version-pinned charts in charts.yaml from operator-configured
# registries (public.ecr.aws, ghcr.io/slinkyproject) during stack
# deploys — not untrusted, attacker-supplied artifacts — and the Lambda
# runs only at deploy time, not as a long-lived network service.
#
# Clears when a helm release ships with oras-go >= v2.6.2: bump the helm
# pin + SHA256 trust anchor in lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile, the
# HELM_VERSION/HELM_SHA256 env pairs in integration-tests.yml and
# deps-scan.yml, and the expected pins in tests/test_supply_chain_integrity.py,
# then drop this entry.
# Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-50163
CVE-2026-50163 exp:2026-09-22

# CVE-2025-47273 (setuptools 70.3.0, fixed 78.1.1) and GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
# (msgpack 1.1.2, fixed 1.2.1) — both live only inside the AWS CLI v2
# vendored bundle (usr/local/aws-cli/v2/2.36.19/dist/) in the dev image,
# not in our resolvable dependency tree (msgpack appears nowhere in
# requirements-lock.txt, and site-packages setuptools is current).
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: 2.36.19 is the newest AWS CLI v2 release
# (this PR just bumped to it); the vendored versions are chosen by the AWS
# CLI's own build. Clears when a newer AWS CLI release rebuilds its bundle
# with patched deps — bump AWSCLI_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev and re-check.
#
# Low runtime risk: the dev image is a contributor/CI tool that is never
# deployed; the flagged code paths run only inside `aws` CLI invocations
# against operator-supplied input.
#
# Advisories: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-47273
#             https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273 exp:2026-10-15
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2026-10-15

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-46600 — golang.org/x/net/dns/dnsmessage panic on crafted SVCB/HTTPS
# resource records (HIGH, fixed in x/net 0.56.0).
#
# Flagged in two pinned Go binaries: helm v4.2.3 in the helm-installer
# image, and kubectl in the dev image. Not clearable by a pin bump today:
# helm v4.2.3 is the newest upstream release (checked 2026-08-12) with no
# rebuild against patched x/net yet. Clears per binary as upstream ships
# rebuilds with x/net >= 0.56.0 — bump the helm pin in
# lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile and the kubectl pin in Dockerfile.dev,
# then re-check both scans.
#
# Low runtime risk: the flaw is in DNS response parsing. The helm-installer
# Lambda only invokes helm against pinned chart registries inside the VPC
# with VPC-provided DNS, and the dev image is a contributor/CI tool that is
# never deployed.
#
# Advisories: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-46600
#             https://access.redhat.com/security/cve/cve-2026-46600
CVE-2026-46600 exp:2026-10-15
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-33818 (encoding/asn1 DoS), CVE-2026-56853 (net/http unencrypted
# HTTP/2), CVE-2026-56858 (html/template XSS), CVE-2026-56859 (encoding/xml
# DoS), CVE-2026-56860 (net/url DoS), CVE-2026-56862 (crypto/tls DoS) — all
# six are Go STDLIB flaws in binaries built with go1.26.5, fixed in Go
# 1.25.13 / 1.26.6 / 1.27.0-rc.3.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the Security workflow was green on
# 29f8aeca (2026-08-15 06:53Z) and red on the next commit three hours later,
# with only chart/manifest/test/doc changes in between — none of which reach
# these images' contents. The Go advisories landed in the Trivy DB in that
# window, so they appeared on unchanged binaries.
#
# Flagged in every scanned image that carries a Go binary:
#   dev (Dockerfile.dev)                    docker, docker-buildx, kubectl
#   helm-installer                          helm, kubectl, aws-lambda-rie
#   tls-certificate-manager                 aws-lambda-rie
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump — each upstream checked 2026-08-15:
#   kubectl        v1.36.3 IS stable and stable-1.36
#                  (https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt), so there is no
#                  newer release to move to.
#   helm           v4.2.4 exists (we pin v4.2.3), but BOTH release binaries
#                  report go1.26.5 via `go version -m`, so the bump would not
#                  clear these findings. Deferred to the next helm release
#                  that is actually rebuilt on a patched Go.
#   docker         29.7.2 is the newest static build on
#                  download.docker.com/linux/static/stable.
#   docker-buildx  v0.36.1 is the latest release.
#   aws-lambda-rie not a pin we own: it ships inside
#                  public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14, the AWS-managed base
#                  image both Lambda images build on. It clears when AWS
#                  republishes that base on a patched Go.
#
# Low runtime risk. Every flagged binary is a short-lived CLI/tooling client,
# never a long-lived network service:
#   * net/http unencrypted HTTP/2 and html/template XSS are server-side
#     surfaces these clients do not expose at all.
#   * the encoding/xml, net/url and crypto/tls denial-of-service paths need
#     hostile input; these binaries talk only to trusted peers — the EKS API
#     endpoint and the version-pinned chart registries in charts.yaml, reached
#     over VPC endpoints — and encoding/asn1 is certificate parsing against
#     those same endpoints.
#   * aws-lambda-rie is the local Runtime Interface Emulator. Deployed Lambda
#     functions get their runtime API from the platform, so the RIE binary is
#     not on the production invocation path; it exists for local invocation.
#   * the dev image is contributor/CI tooling and is never deployed.
#
# Clears per binary as upstream ships rebuilds on Go >= 1.26.6: bump
# KUBECTL_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev plus the kubectl pin in
# lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile, the helm pin + SHA256 there (in lockstep
# with the HELM_VERSION/HELM_SHA256 pairs in integration-tests.yml and
# deps-scan.yml and the expected pins in tests/test_supply_chain_integrity.py),
# ARG DOCKER_VERSION / ARG BUILDX_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev, and the Lambda
# base image tag; then drop the ids that no longer appear.
# Advisories: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-33818 (and the matching
#             nvd/cve-2026-XXXXX page for each id below)
CVE-2026-33818 exp:2026-10-15
CVE-2026-56853 exp:2026-10-15
CVE-2026-56858 exp:2026-10-15
CVE-2026-56859 exp:2026-10-15
CVE-2026-56860 exp:2026-10-15
CVE-2026-56862 exp:2026-10-15
